
An energy pair distribution is the distribution of individual
molecule-molecule interactions; e.g., for water-water interactions
the range would be from about -6.5 kcal/mol (the strongest H-bond) to
ca. +3 kcal/mol in liquid water. This is computed as a histogram. The
units for the y-axis are # of molecules/(kcal/mol).

A full energy distribution records the distribution of the total
interaction energy for a molecule with all other molecules. This is
often rather Gaussian in shape. The mean value is the average energy
that it would take to transfer one molecule out of the liquid and into the
gas phase; it is also roughly twice the negative of the energy of vaporization
for a pure liquid. Its units are mole fraction/(kcal/mol).

